rules.requirement:
A landlord of a unit covered by the Rent Ordinance may evict only for one of 17 just causes, and that cause must be the dominant motive. Expiration of a rental agreement or a change in ownership is not just cause.

The causes are:
- non-payment or habitual late payment of rent, or frequent bounced checks;
- failure to cure a substantial breach;
- severe, continuing or recurring nuisance;
- illegal use, excluding mere occupancy of an unwarranted unit or a single cured short-term rental violation;
- refusal to sign a materially similar renewal;
- refusal of lawful access;
- an unapproved subtenant as the only remaining occupant;
- owner or limited relative move-in;
- sale under an approved condominium conversion;
- demolition or permanent removal from housing use;
- temporary capital improvement or rehabilitation work, with the tenant's right to reoccupy;
- substantial rehabilitation of a building at least 50 years old where the work costs at least 75% of new construction;
- Ellis Act withdrawal of all units;
- lead remediation under Health Code Arts. 11 or 26;
- demolition under a Chapter 56 development agreement;
- expiry of a Good Samaritan occupancy agreement, with the eviction notice served within 60 days;
- demolition for a development project under Planning Code § 317.

Just cause is also required to remove housing services such as parking, storage and laundry. A temporary removal is allowed for mandatory soft-story seismic retrofit work under § 37.2(r).

A landlord who serves a notice of termination for one of the following causes must pay relocation to the tenants:
- owner or relative move-in;
- demolition or permanent removal of the unit from housing use;
- temporary capital improvement work;
- substantial rehabilitation.

The amount is set per tenant and capped per unit. An additional amount is due for each elderly (60+) or disabled tenant, or for a household with minor children. Amounts adjust each March 1 and depend on the date the notice is served. Relocation for temporary capital improvement evictions of less than 20 days is governed by Cal. Civ. Code § 1947.9, not § 37.9C.
